From 57023243dc5285a023940d9a3d328574efe549a0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 03:46:01 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 112/119] docs: the EXR tap becomes measurable, and an EXR
 sequence out of TrackView

CSceneReferredExport had no PROFILE_LABEL_SCOPE at all, so its cost could only be
inferred: the negative blit was an unnamed copy and the readback's staging copy was
invisible. SCENE_REFERRED_EXPORT_TAP now wraps everything the exporter does on the GPU
and SCENE_REFERRED_EXPORT_NEGATIVE names the full-res blit inside it. The GRADED tap
still has no marker and cannot have one - it is an extra SV_Target1 write inside the
TONEMAPPING pass, so its cost shows as a widening of TONEMAPPING; the comment says so.

docs/SceneReferredExport.md section 7: how to drive rec_CaptureEXR / rec_CaptureEXRStop
from a TrackView Console track (CAnimSceneNode::ApplyConsoleKey is a plain
ExecuteString, so no engine work was needed), that an 8-bit TrackView capture and an
EXR capture can run in the same playthrough and number independently, and the three
limits worth knowing before losing a take:

  - the batch-render dialog's per-item cvar list runs BEFORE its ResizeContext, so a
    rec_CaptureEXR typed there arms and dies on the same frame. Use a Console key.
  - a non-capture sequence carrying a TimeWarp or FixedTimeStep track zeroes t_FixedStep
    every frame and will silently take the cadence off an armed export. Not guarded
    against today, and now written down rather than rediscovered.
  - e_ScreenShot 1 resizes the pipeline twice and therefore ends an armed capture, and
    at 4096 square costs about +340 MiB over stock for one nested frame.

Notes: research/s8-output-paths.md, research/s8-perf-and-permutations.md section 2.1.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01A72MFkdSczncE7au9UVnmu
